These are my notes from learning how to build an OS from scratch.

====
So in the first part, we gonna use a pre-configured bootloader.
In this section we gonna use the GRUB bootloader

https://www.gnu.org/software/grub/manual/grub/grub.html

We could use Limine or a custom bootloader. But first we gonna start with GRUB.
====


----
In this firt part we gonna start by writing HELLO WORLD on a simple kernel.

To start it. We need to tell GRUB how to use our C code.

The first things we need to write is an assembly code that do that.
Why? because it is the entry point.

the first thing that we create in this file is a multiboot section to `show`
GRUB that yeah... pls use me use me... or something like that. But generally we need that header
If we were to use Limine or anything else we would refer to their API documentation but here we just first
define a section that is a .multiboot

then, We align it on 4 bytes because it is mandatory. That's what we NEED to do.
and we define doubleword the magic number used in GRUB that is actually 0x1BADB002.

Define Doubleword is actually a keyword to allocate 4 bytes ( 32bits ) in NASM.

GRUB will search for that magic number before doing anything... so that is, logically, the first things we define

Then we proceed to do some checksum.

How? -> it will follow this rules `magic + flags + checksum = 0`
AND MUST BE LIKE THAT.
the flags is 0x00, the magic number is yeah... the magic number and the checksum is actually the number that we define doubleword.
so it will be like checksum = -magic + flags
We can also dd the flags before doing the checksum

The result must be 0. Either... GRUB will think it is a corrupted file.


The next section is the block started by Symbol section.
What is that ?
A simple section to auto-initialize everything at 0.
So let's say I define something inside that section... logically... when we gonna use that it will be auto-0-ed

What will be inside that section?
We will align 16 bytes... because it is like that... did'nt remember WHY. But pls use the docs if you are not ME.
We need a stack ( that's fucking logic )

What we gonna do is first define the bottom of the stack... call it anything you want.
And then reserver 16 Kbyte... or more accuratly 16384.

then initialize the top of the stack...

It is useless to fill the memory manually because those 16384 will be initilize auto because they are in the bss section.

I never tried initializing the top before the bottom but it should work as well.

Then we need a text section. What is that? it is just a section for code... any code SHOULD and WILL be put there.
define it with global and call it whatever the fuck you want but I recommend using _start
and also... specify that the function you gonna use is located outise so like in C use extern and specify the main function name.

remember we difened the _start stuff... it is like a function in some other language... what we gonna do here in defining what will happen inside of it.
inside of it... we gonna to some stuff.

The first things I do is:
  * initializing the stack pointer to be it tha top of the stack.
  * NOT USE interuption because we didn't define any ITP yet... or idk what is it called but it will come... so call cli ( clear Interrupt )
  * call the main C function.
  * you CAN call halt - to stop the CPU usage. ( like freezing )
  * you can also jump to the very start of the function using the $ sign... that point to the current address of the stuff we use.

We don't define the main C file yet...

But let's look at the linker ... so we keep hard stuff for later

We define the linker in a .ld file.

We define what is the ENTRY point. by pointing at the _start things.
We define the SECTIONS ( for memory disposition )
  * load the kernel at the 1Mo address. ( GRUB will handle it auto so don't do 2Mo, but i'm not sure yet ) ( . = 1M )
  * use . to point the current memory.
  * load the multiboot first. and ALIGN 4 byte to it and then load every .multiboot by doing *(.multiboot) to merge every multiboot section of every output file
  * load the code and ALIGN 4Kilobyte for it. OFC merge.
  * load the data and merge ( 4Kb too )
  * load the bss and merge.

We use 4Ko because it is the standar for page memory on x86_64
!!! it is important for pagination later...
